{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 45, :comments ["<b>So all those numbered of the children of Israel, by their fathers' houses, etc. — all who go forth to war in Israel</b> – Scripture needed to state the total number after it had stated the details, for Moses and Aaron were commanded to know the number of the census of the people and to know the number of each tribe, for such is the way of kings when they count their people. Now I do not understand the reason for this commandment — why the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded it. For there was a need for them to be registered by their tribes on account of the banners, but as for knowing the number, I do not know why He commanded them to know it. Perhaps it was to make known to them His lovingkindness toward them, for with seventy souls their fathers went down to Egypt, and now they are like the sand of the sea — so many and so many thousands aged twenty and upward. And after every pestilence and plague He counts them, to make known that He increases the nations and when He strikes, His hands weaken — and this is what our Sages said: out of the abundance of His love for them He counts them at every moment. Furthermore, one who comes before the father of prophets and his brother the holy one of HaShem, and is known to them by name, will thereby merit virtue and life, for he enters into the assembly of the people and into the register of the children of Israel, and the merit of the multitudes lies in their number. And likewise they all gain merit through the counting that takes place before Moses and Aaron, for they will set their eyes upon them for good and seek mercy upon them: \"HaShem, the Elohim of your fathers, add to you a thousand times as many as you are\" (Deuteronomy 1:11), and He will not diminish their number, and the half-shekel atones for your souls. And in Midbar Sinai Rabbah I saw as follows: \"By the number of names, according to their persons\" — the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: count them with honor and grandeur, each and every individual. You should not say to the head of the family: \"How many are in your family?\" \"How many children do you have?\" but rather all of them shall pass before you in awe and honor, and you count them. That is what is written: \"By the number of names, from twenty years old and upward, according to their persons\" (Numbers 1:18). And it is also possible to say further that this was according to the practice of the kingdom when they come to war, for now they were ready to enter the land and to engage in battle with the kings of the Amorites across the Jordan and with all the rest of them, as He said: \"We are setting out to the place of which HaShem said\" (Numbers 10:29), and Moses and the princes needed to know the number of those armed for battle, and likewise the number of each and every tribe, and what he would assign to them in the plains of Moab in battle formations, for the Torah does not rely on a miracle that one should pursue a thousand. And this is the meaning of <b>all who go forth to war in Israel</b>, for the counting was on account of the army of battle. And further, so that He may apportion the land to them according to their number and know how many districts will fall to them from the land conquered for them, for were it not for the matter of the spies, they would have entered there immediately."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The Aramaic-style quotation from Midbar Sinai Rabbah is rendered retaining its declarative character ('הדא הוא דכתיב' rendered as 'That is what is written'). The phrase 'ימחץ וידיו תרפינה' is rendered hyper-literally; it alludes to Deuteronomy 32:36 but the syntax is elliptical in the original."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:02:24.520862271Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}